<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Which package to exercise and which of its target frameworks. Both go through properties of
      our own: overriding TargetFramework directly on the command line breaks restore, and the two
      RTC packages cannot be referenced together — they carry the same Java classes, so one app
      holds one of them. The other four products could technically coexist with an RTC package
      (different Java packages, different — or no — native libraries), but each stays its own
      flavor anyway so that its failure cannot hide behind, or be caused by, another product's
      much larger payload. One product per app, always.

      IoT is deliberately not a flavor: io.agora:iotsdk bundles whole private copies of the RTC and
      RTM SDKs, so it is exclusive with everything, and only io.agora.iotlink is bound.
    -->
    <AgoraDevicePackage Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == '' ">Video</AgoraDevicePackage>
    <AgoraDeviceTargetFramework Condition=" '$(AgoraDeviceTargetFramework)' == '' ">net9.0-android35.0</AgoraDeviceTargetFramework>
    <TargetFramework>$(AgoraDeviceTargetFramework)</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      One define per flavor, and one suite per define: each flavor's file declares the whole
      SmokeTests class, so MainActivity needs no flavor awareness at all.

      AGORA_RTC covers Video and Voice together, because those two share a suite (SmokeTests.cs)
      rather than having one each. It is a positive define rather than the "everything that is not
      Signaling" it used to be: with six flavors, the negative form silently compiled the RTC suite
      into every product that had not thought to exclude itself, and the first symptom was a page
      of CS0246s about Agora.Rtc.

      AGORA_VOICE then narrows within that suite. The voice artifact is the same engine with the
      video pipeline stripped at the native layer; the Java API surface still declares the video
      entry points, so the video calls compile against it and merely answer error codes that vary
      by call. Compiling them out rather than tolerating those codes keeps a video failure on the
      Video package a hard failure.
    -->
    <DefineConstants Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Video' Or '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Voice' ">$(DefineConstants);AGORA_RTC</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Voice' ">$(DefineConstants);AGORA_VOICE</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Signaling' ">$(DefineConstants);AGORA_SIGNALING</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Chat' ">$(DefineConstants);AGORA_CHAT</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Whiteboard' ">$(DefineConstants);AGORA_WHITEBOARD</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Fastboard' ">$(DefineConstants);AGORA_FASTBOARD</DefineConstants>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <CheckEolTargetFramework>false</CheckEolTargetFramework>
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>

    <ApplicationId>com.sbokatuk.agora.android.devicetests</ApplicationId>
    <ApplicationVersion>1</ApplicationVersion>
    <ApplicationDisplayVersion>1.0</ApplicationDisplayVersion>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>23</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>

    <!--
      The property, not an @(AndroidManifest) item, and an explicit file rather than none at all.
      The .NET 9 and .NET 10 Android SDKs generate a manifest when the project has no
      $(AndroidManifest); the .NET 8 one defaults the property to the project root and then fails
      because nothing is there:

          error XA1018: Specified AndroidManifest file does not exist: .../AndroidManifest.xml

      The API level in the target framework decides which SDK band builds the app, and
      net8.0-android34.0 — an e2e matrix leg — is the .NET 8 band.
    -->
    <AndroidManifest>AndroidManifest.xml</AndroidManifest>
    <AndroidPackageFormat>apk</AndroidPackageFormat>

    <!--
      No Java shrinking, no managed trimming and no AOT, in any configuration.

      run-emulator-tests.sh builds this in Debug, where none of the three happen anyway; these are
      here so that building it in Release by hand behaves the same rather than failing in two
      confusing ways:

        - R8 legitimately removes Java classes nothing references. The entry-point check below
          reaches Agora's classes only through Class.forName, which R8 cannot see, so a shrunk
          build reports them missing when the package is in fact correct.
        - AOT bakes type layouts from the assembly set it was given. With trimming off, the image
          disagrees with what the runtime loads and the app aborts on startup before any check
          runs, with nothing in the message to suggest a build configuration.

      A failure here should only ever mean "the .aar is not in the app" or "the binding does not
      drive the SDK", which are the two things this suite is meant to be able to say.

      -p:AgoraShrinkTest=true is the deliberate exception: it turns Java shrinking back ON (and
      only Java shrinking — managed trimming and AOT stay off) so one leg exercises the
      configuration a store build actually ships with: R8 plus the auto-generated bound-surface
      keeps plus whatever rules the package carries (in-aar proguard.txt for RTC, packed
      proguard/ + buildTransitive/ for the products whose aars ship none — see
      src/Agora.Proguard.targets). A regression in any of those layers surfaces here as the
      Class.forName entry-point checks failing or the SDK dying at runtime.
    -->
    <AndroidLinkTool Condition=" '$(AgoraShrinkTest)' != 'true' "></AndroidLinkTool>
    <AndroidLinkTool Condition=" '$(AgoraShrinkTest)' == 'true' ">r8</AndroidLinkTool>
    <AndroidLinkMode>None</AndroidLinkMode>
    <RunAOTCompilation>false</RunAOTCompilation>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>android-arm64;android-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The e2e deliberately consumes the packed NuGet from ./artifacts rather than a ProjectReference,
    so it exercises the artifact that actually gets published — including the merged net10 assets,
    which only exist in the package (see build/merge-packages.py) and which a ProjectReference
    would prove nothing about. See NuGet.config for the local feed.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoraDevicePackageVersion Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)' == '' And '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Voice' ">$(AgoraVoiceVersion).$(AgoraVoiceBindingRevision)</AgoraDevicePackageVersion>
    <AgoraDevicePackageVersion Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)' == '' And '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Signaling' ">$(AgoraSignalingVersion).$(AgoraSignalingBindingRevision)</AgoraDevicePackageVersion>
    <AgoraDevicePackageVersion Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)' == '' And '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Chat' ">$(AgoraChatVersion).$(AgoraChatBindingRevision)</AgoraDevicePackageVersion>
    <AgoraDevicePackageVersion Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)' == '' And '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Whiteboard' ">$(AgoraWhiteboardVersion).$(AgoraWhiteboardBindingRevision)</AgoraDevicePackageVersion>
    <AgoraDevicePackageVersion Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)' == '' And '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Fastboard' ">$(AgoraFastboardVersion).$(AgoraFastboardBindingRevision)</AgoraDevicePackageVersion>
    <AgoraDevicePackageVersion Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)' == '' ">$(AgoraVideoVersion).$(AgoraVideoBindingRevision)</AgoraDevicePackageVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Exact version, in brackets — a bare version is a *minimum* to NuGet, so a build missing from
      artifacts/ would silently resolve from nuget.org instead of failing the restore.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Net.Agora.$(AgoraDevicePackage).Android" Version="[$(AgoraDevicePackageVersion)]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Fastboard only. Its transitive graph asks for two mutually-exclusive
    Xamarin.AndroidX.Lifecycle.Common ranges and NuGet cannot unify them, so restore fails outright
    with NU1107 before anything builds:

        Fastboard.Android -> Whiteboard.Android -> WebKit -> Core 1.16.0.3
            -> Lifecycle.Runtime 2.9.2.1 -> Lifecycle.Common [>= 2.9.2.1 && < 2.9.3]
        Fastboard.Android -> AppCompat -> Fragment -> LiveData.Core 2.8.7.4
            -> Lifecycle.Common [>= 2.8.7.4 && < 2.8.8]

    Pinning the newer one directly is exactly what NU1107 asks for, and a direct reference outranks
    every transitive vote. Scoped to Fastboard: the Whiteboard flavor does not pull the AppCompat
    side of the diamond, so its restore is clean and must not be perturbed. The facade repository's
    device tests carry the same pin for the same reason — this is a property of the package graph,
    not of either test app.

    It trades the NU1107 error for a handful of NU1608 warnings, because AppCompat's own
    Lifecycle.Process and Lifecycle.LiveData.Core still declare the 2.8.x upper bound they were
    published against. That is the documented cost of the direct-reference fix; chasing it with
    further pins only moves the warning to the next package in the graph.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(AgoraDevicePackage)' == 'Fastboard' ">
    <PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Lifecycle.Common" Version="2.9.2.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A typo in -p:AgoraDevicePackage otherwise fails as a page of CS0246s (no suite defines
    SmokeTests, because no flavor define was set) or, worse, as a restore error about a package
    that never existed. Say what actually went wrong instead.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoraDeviceFlavors>;Video;Voice;Signaling;Chat;Whiteboard;Fastboard;</AgoraDeviceFlavors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="_AgoraValidateDevicePackage" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild">
    <Error Condition="!$(AgoraDeviceFlavors.Contains(';$(AgoraDevicePackage);'))"
           Text="AgoraDevicePackage='$(AgoraDevicePackage)' is not a device test flavor. Pick one of: $(AgoraDeviceFlavors.Trim(';').Replace(';', ', '))." />
  </Target>

</Project>
